# a frame without an owner: the header name is also its name
ok root name applied
ok alone: plain header name is the instance
ok alone: qualified header name reaches its own child
ok alone: qualified forward reference fixed up in the instance
alone: Title.Buddy=FooterFrame Note.Buddy=Title Buddy=Note
# two instances in a form: each file reference names its own instance
ok instance names from the form
ok F1: plain header name is the instance
ok F1: qualified header name reaches its own child
ok F1: qualified forward reference fixed up in the instance
F1: Title.Buddy=F1 Note.Buddy=Title Buddy=Note
ok F2: plain header name is the instance
ok F2: qualified header name reaches its own child
ok F2: qualified forward reference fixed up in the instance
F2: Title.Buddy=F2 Note.Buddy=Title Buddy=Note
ok the global component of the same name is not used
# frames created in code with an owner
ok both keep an empty name
ok X: plain header name is the instance
ok X: qualified header name reaches its own child
ok X: qualified forward reference fixed up in the instance
X: Title.Buddy=<TFooterFrame> Note.Buddy=Title Buddy=Note
ok Y: plain header name is the instance
ok Y: qualified header name reaches its own child
ok Y: qualified forward reference fixed up in the instance
Y: Title.Buddy=<TFooterFrame> Note.Buddy=Title Buddy=Note
# in another file the frame's header name is not a name of that file
ok plain header name of the frame in the form
ok qualified one goes through FindGlobalComponent
